um  now  another question here last one i'm gonna  do  uh can athletes also from adam by the  way can athletes get around the negative  effects of high sugar consumption  because they are more active uh the  short answer is yes  so  a lot of the met like cardio metabolic  concerns about sugar are largely  related to very high intakes of fructose  aside from just being in very positive  energy balance  but fructose does get a little bit more  consideration a little bit more  attention because fructose is  metabolized uh differently than other  monosaccharides so  fructose uh is mostly metabolized in the  liver which can definitely cause some  issues uh and one of the issues is  there's kind of just like this  bottleneck effect of all these  substrates  going to the liver um you know just this  overload of you know carbohydrate and de  novo lipogenesis occurring there and uh  being in an energy surplus with a  tremendous amount of fructose that's all  kind of going to the liver and not  causing uh great things when it comes to  cardio metabolic health  now  the reason i bring that up is because if  you are physically active and exercising  you know when we look at sedentary folks  that are on a  a very high fructose diet we will see  hepatic  de novo lipogenesis so de novo  lipogenesis in the liver we'll see  increases in  fat storage within the liver  we'll see increases in blood trigly  triglyceride concentrations  and  also an impairment of insulin  sensitivity so those are the types of  cardiometabolic issues that we're  looking at  that's in sedentary folks who are in  positive energy balance with a  pretty notable influx of  really deliberately high fructose  if you are  active exercising that is clearing a lot  of those substrates out of your liver  there's a lot of energy flux going on  substrates are coming in they're being  utilized that's a good thing  substrates come in substrates go out  can't explain that you can't uh and and  i won't  but  there there is a study um that kind of  looked at this exact question um  and if anything the study undersells uh  the safety mechanisms for the typical  healthy active person but the study was  by smash and colleagues in 2020 titled  metabolic effects of a prolonged very  high dose dietary fructose challenge and  healthy subjects and that's when you  know that it's a pretty serious  intervention when they're not even  calling it a high fructose diet it's a  high fructose challenge that means the  fructose is pretty high so in this  particular study they were looking at  150 grams of fructose daily  which would be like 300 grams of table  sugar on a daily basis  the fructose equivalent  uh  looked at 10 healthy subjects  after eight weeks of adhering to this  that's a pretty substantial amount of  time  to have that much fructose and these are  are healthy folks but they're not like  you know  running 15 miles a week or anything like  that just healthy folks who are not  deliberately eating in a you know a  caloric surplus  and  basically body weight decreased slightly  during the study  none of the outcomes they looked at  related to  the liver or metabolic health were  significantly impacted in a negative way  so if you have um you know  if your body composition is within a  healthy range for you  and you're  quite active and you're not in a large  energy surplus  you know  dealing with sugar even if it's pure  fructose in this example doesn't seem to  be uh  much of a challenge at all doesn't seem  to be much of a problem so  uh esp for someone who's relatively lean  and exercising regularly uh it is really  not something that is worth uh  being too concerned about as long as  sugar's not displacing other important  stuff from your diet but you know the  the anecdotal evidence for this is quite  abundant as well um  i don't know many people who have more  sugar than the typical endurance athlete  who's putting in pretty high mileage um  you know we're not seeing a lot of  endurance athletes coming in with you  know non-alcoholic fatty liver disease  or you know metabolic syndrome or things  like that so absolutely  if you're active and especially if you  maintain relatively lean body  composition and you're not in a huge  caloric surplus  you can certainly get around some of  those negative effects of high sugar  consumption

